3.1 In Lean Buffer
==================

Key           Command                     Effect
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘C-c C-e’     ‘eglot’                     Start Eglot, the LSP client
‘C-c C-r’     ‘lena-eglot-restart-file’   Reprocess the file, building out-of-date imports first
‘C-c C-i’     ‘lena-infoview’             Toggle the infoview (needs Lean 4.30 or later)
‘C-c C-c’     ‘project-compile’           Build the project; suggests ‘lake build’
‘C-h .’       ‘eldoc-doc-buffer’          Show goals and expected type, when there's no infoview
‘C-\’         ‘toggle-input-method’       Turn the ‘Lena’ input method off and on
‘C-u C-x =’   ‘describe-char’             Describe the character at point, and how to type it
‘M-g i’       ‘imenu’                     Jump to a definition in the buffer

   The ‘Lena’ input method is on in Lean buffers, shown as ‘∃∀’ in the
mode line: For example, ‘\a’ gives ‘α’, ‘\to’ gives ‘→’, and ‘\\’ a
backslash.


File: lena.info,  Node: In the Infoview,  Next: Signs in the Margin,  Prev: In Lean Buffer,  Up: Usage

3.2 In the Infoview
===================

The infoview follows point and shows the goals, the expected type and
Lean's messages on the current line.  While Lean builds the imports, it
shows Lake's output.

Key                       Command                        Effect
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘n’ / ‘p’                 ‘lena-infoview-next-goal’      Next / previous goal
‘w’                       ‘lena-infoview-copy-goal’      Copy the goal
‘v’                       ‘lena-infoview-view-options’   Menu of what the infoview shows
‘i’ ‘t’ ‘a’ ‘l’ ‘r’ ‘d’                                  Toggle instances, types, inaccessible names, let values, target first, diffs
‘TAB’                                                    Fold a heading, or expand a trace
‘RET’, ‘mouse-1’                                         Press a button, like ‘[apply]’, or go to a message's position

   On a subterm of a goal:

Key                         Command                    Effect
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘RET’, ‘mouse-1’, ‘C-h .’   ‘lena-infoview-describe’   Describe it in ‘*Lena Help*’: explicit form, type, docs
‘M-.’ / ‘M-,’               ‘xref-find-definitions’    Go to its definition / come back

   Hovering a subterm shows its explicit form and type with ElDoc (in a
popup with eldoc-box (https://github.com/casouri/eldoc-box)) and, with
‘tooltip-mode’, in a tooltip; setting ‘use-system-tooltips’ to ‘nil’
formats these better.  In ‘*Lena Help*’, ‘l’ and ‘r’ go back and forth.


File: lena.info,  Node: Signs in the Margin,  Next: With Evil,  Prev: In the Infoview,  Up: Usage

3.3 Signs in the Margin
=======================

While Eglot manages the buffer, ‘lena-margin-mode’ shows signs in the
left margin in place of Flymake's indicators.  Hovering a sign shows its
message; clicking it lists all diagnostics.

Sign          Meaning
-------------------------------------------------------
‘‼’ / ‘!’     Error / warning or note on this line
‘┌’ ‘│’ ‘└’   A diagnostic spanning several lines
‘✓’           Complete proof
‘✗’           Unsolved goals; the proof goes on here
‘⚠’           Declaration that uses ‘sorry’
orange ‘│’    Lean is still processing these lines


File: lena.info,  Node: With Evil,  Prev: Signs in the Margin,  Up: Usage

3.4 With Evil
=============

With the ‘lena-evil’ package, ‘(with-eval-after-load 'evil
(lena-evil-setup))’ binds these keys in the infoview's normal state:

Key                 Effect
----------------------------------------------
‘]g’ / ‘[g’         Next / previous goal
‘]h’ / ‘[h’         Next / previous heading
‘gr’                Refresh
‘K’                 Describe the subterm
‘<localleader> v’   View options
‘<localleader> y’   Copy the goal

   In ‘*Lena Help*’, ‘C-o’ and ‘C-i’ go back and forth.  In Doom Emacs,
‘K’, ‘gd’ and ‘C-o’ describe, go to a definition, and come back.


